      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best bedroom humidifier is the one you forget is running: quiet enough not to wake you, dark enough not to light the room, and easy enough to clean that it never smells. Most people need a small ultrasonic unit and distilled water; people with hard water should go evaporative.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose These Six
          </h2>

          <p>
            A bedroom humidifier has a narrower job than a living-room one, and a harder one. It runs while you are asleep, a few feet from your head, in a closed room that is usually between 120 and 300 square feet. That means noise, light and over-humidification matter more than raw output, and a machine that is a chore to clean will end up growing something in its tank within a couple of weeks of neglect. We built this list around those four problems rather than around the biggest tank or the highest mist rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every specification on this page comes from the maker's product page, owner's manual or the retail listing, and we say so as we go. Where sources disagree, which happens often with humidifier run times, we give the range and tell you which number to plan around. Where a manufacturer does not publish a figure, such as a noise rating for an evaporative fan, we say that instead of guessing. None of the decibel or run-time figures below are our own measurements; they are the published ratings, and you should read them that way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Four criteria decided the shortlist. First, a published noise rating at or below about 30 dB for ultrasonic machines, or a low fan setting for evaporative ones. Second, a sleep mode or a way to turn every light off, because a glowing display is a surprisingly common reason people stop using a bedroom humidifier. Third, a tank sized to last at least one full night on a low setting in a typical bedroom. Fourth, a design you can clean in a few minutes: top-fill tanks, wide openings, or dishwasher-safe parts.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also deliberately mixed technologies. Four picks are ultrasonic, which are quieter but spread white dust with hard water, and two are evaporative, which are louder but self-limiting and dust-free. Which one suits you depends on your water and how you sleep, and the sections after the picks walk through that choice. If you want the broader comparison, our <a href="/evaporative-vs-ultrasonic-humidifier/">evaporative vs ultrasonic humidifier guide</a> covers it in depth, and our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier guide</a> ranks machines for every room in the house, not just the bedroom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Noise: Why Ultrasonic Usually Wins at Night
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise is the first thing that separates a good bedroom humidifier from a bad one, and the technology decides most of it. An ultrasonic humidifier uses a small ceramic plate vibrating at a frequency you cannot hear to break water into a fine mist, and a small fan pushes that mist out of the nozzle. With no large fan, the main sounds are a faint hum and the occasional gurgle as water moves from the tank into the reservoir. That is why the quietest published ratings on this list, 28 dB for the Dreo HM311 and the Levoit Dual 200S, both belong to ultrasonic machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier works differently. A wick or paper filter soaks up water from the tank, and a fan blows room air through it, so moisture leaves as invisible vapor. The fan is the whole mechanism, so you will always hear it. On low it is a soft, steady whoosh, which many people find soothing and some actively use as white noise. On high it is loud enough that most people will not want it running beside the bed. Manufacturers of evaporative units rarely publish decibel ratings, which is itself a hint that they are not competing on silence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Context matters as much as the number. A 30 dB rating is roughly the level of a quiet whisper, and in a bedroom with a running furnace or a ceiling fan you will not hear it at all. In a silent room in the middle of the night, even a soft gurgle can be noticeable to a light sleeper. If you are sensitive to irregular sounds, an ultrasonic machine with a top-fill tank tends to gurgle less than an old bottle-style tank that glugs as the water level drops. If you find steady noise relaxing, an evaporative fan on low may actually help you sleep.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical tips cut noise on any machine. Set it on a firm, level surface, because a wobbly nightstand turns small vibrations into a rattle. And run it on its lowest setting overnight, with a higher setting earlier in the evening to bring the room up to target before you go to bed. A model with an automatic humidity mode does this for you, ramping down once the room is humid enough, which is one reason our top pick earns its place.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
