        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing a Humidifier to Your Bedroom
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bedrooms are smaller than the rooms humidifiers are marketed for, which is good news: you rarely need a large machine, and an oversized one in a small closed room is how windows end up streaming with condensation. Start with the floor area. A typical secondary bedroom is around 100 to 150 square feet, a standard primary bedroom 200 to 300, and a large primary suite 300 to 450. Match that against the maker's coverage figure, and treat the figure as a ceiling rather than a target.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage ratings assume an average ceiling height of about 8 feet and a reasonably tight room. Vaulted ceilings, a drafty older house, an open door to a hallway or a forced-air heating system running constantly all increase how much moisture the room needs. If two of those apply, size up one step. If the room is small and closed with the door shut all night, size down, or use the lowest setting. Our <a href="/what-size-humidifier-do-i-need/">humidifier sizing guide</a> goes through the math for every room in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run time is the other half of sizing. What you actually want is a tank that lasts a full night on the setting you will use, with some margin. The manufacturer's longest run time is always on the lowest setting, so a machine rated for 25 hours on low might last 10 to 12 hours on a medium setting in a very dry room. That is why we lean towards 3 to 6 liters for most bedrooms: a smaller tank means a nightly refill, and a larger one means refilling every two or three days.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your bedroom is unusually large or opens onto a sitting area, you are really shopping for a living-room machine, and our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-large-room/">best humidifier for large rooms</a> guide is the better starting point. For anything under about 300 square feet, one of the compact picks above, especially the Levoit Dual 200S or Dreo HM311, is the right size.
          </p>

