        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where to Put It: Distance From the Bed and Off the Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Placement decides whether a humidifier helps or causes problems. The most common mistake is putting it on the nightstand with the nozzle pointing straight at the bed. Mist that lands on bedding, a headboard or your face before it evaporates leaves things damp, and damp bedding is uncomfortable at best and a mold problem at worst. Aim for a few feet of distance between the nozzle and the bed, and point the mist into open air rather than at you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get it off the floor. Mist released at floor level often settles on the carpet before it can mix with the room air, leaving a damp patch around the unit, especially with ultrasonic machines on a high setting. A dresser, a sturdy table or a nightstand on the far side of the room works well. If you do place it on the floor, use a waterproof tray underneath and keep it on the lowest setting. Evaporative units are less fussy about height because they release vapor rather than droplets, but they still benefit from a raised, level spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep it away from walls, windows and wood furniture. A nozzle that sprays at a wall will eventually stain paint or wallpaper, and mist that settles on a wooden dresser can raise the grain or leave white rings. Give the unit a foot or more of clearance from the wall and aim it towards the middle of the room. Windows deserve special attention in winter: cold glass is where excess humidity shows up first as condensation, and the frame is where mold starts if that happens every night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about electrical safety and children. Run the cord where nobody will trip on it, and keep the unit out of reach of small children and pets. Warm-mist humidifiers heat water to boiling and are a burn risk on a low nightstand in a child's room, which is one reason most pediatric guidance favors cool-mist units. Our <a href="/warm-mist-vs-cool-mist-humidifier/">warm mist vs cool mist humidifier</a> guide covers that trade-off in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sleep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Humidity and Sleep: Dry Mouth, Congestion and Snoring
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people buy a bedroom humidifier because they wake up with a dry mouth, a scratchy throat, cracked lips or a stuffy nose, and those symptoms track closely with dry winter air. When outdoor air is cold, it holds very little moisture, and heating it indoors drops the relative humidity sharply, sometimes into the teens in a heated bedroom. Adding moisture back into that air is what a humidifier does, and many people find it makes a noticeable difference to how they feel in the morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            The target range matters more than the machine. The EPA and most health organizations recommend keeping indoor relative humidity between about 30 and 50 percent. Below that, dry-air symptoms are more common; above it, dust mites and mold thrive, which is the opposite of what someone with allergies wants. In very cold weather, aim for the lower end of that range, because humid air against cold windows condenses. Our <a href="/ideal-indoor-humidity/">ideal indoor humidity guide</a> explains how to adjust the target as the temperature outside drops.
          </p>

          <p>
            A humidifier is not a treatment for anything, and we are careful not to sell it as one. It can make dry air more comfortable, which may ease some congestion and dryness. It will not fix snoring caused by sleep apnea, a deviated septum or other medical causes, and if you have persistent dry mouth, loud snoring, or wake up gasping, that is worth raising with a doctor rather than solving with an appliance. Mouth breathing at night is a common cause of dry mouth, and a humidifier helps with the dryness without addressing why it happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            If allergies are part of the picture, keep humidity below 50 percent and keep the humidifier scrupulously clean, because a dirty tank can release the very things you are trying to avoid. A good air purifier often helps more than a humidifier for allergy symptoms; our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/">best air purifier for allergies</a> guide covers that. And the only way to know your actual humidity is to measure it: a basic digital hygrometer costs little, and our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">best hygrometer</a> guide picks the accurate ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="white-dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. White Dust and Why Distilled Water Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            White dust is the single most common complaint about ultrasonic humidifiers, and it is entirely about your water. Tap water contains dissolved minerals, mostly calcium and magnesium. An ultrasonic humidifier turns water into a mist without separating those minerals out, so when the droplets evaporate in the air, the minerals are left behind as a fine powder. It settles on furniture, electronics and floors as a gray-white film, and the harder your water, the more of it you will see.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distilled water is the fix. It has had nearly all of its minerals removed, so there is nothing left behind when the mist evaporates. It also means less mineral scale inside the tank and on the ultrasonic plate, which makes cleaning easier and extends the life of the machine. Most ultrasonic manufacturers recommend distilled or demineralized water in their manuals for exactly this reason. The cost is a few dollars a week for a bedroom unit, which is the real running cost of an ultrasonic humidifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some ultrasonic models include a demineralization cartridge, and filtered pitcher water reduces some minerals, but neither fully replaces distilled water. Reverse-osmosis water is close to distilled and works well if you already have an RO system. Softened water is not a substitute: a softener swaps calcium for sodium, which still leaves residue behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Evaporative humidifiers, including the Canopy and Honeywell picks above, avoid white dust differently. The minerals stay in the wick or filter as the water evaporates, so only pure vapor reaches the room. That is why those filters discolor over time and need replacing. If you have hard water and do not want to buy distilled water, an evaporative unit is the simplest answer; if you prefer the quiet of ultrasonic, budget for distilled water from the start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lights" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Night Lights, Displays and Sleep Mode
          </h2>

          <p>
            A humidifier's lights are easy to overlook in a store and impossible to ignore at 2 a.m. A bright blue display, a glowing water-level indicator or a built-in night light can be enough to disrupt sleep in a dark room, and many otherwise good humidifiers end up turned off because of it. Before you buy, check whether every light can be switched off, not just dimmed, and whether the setting stays off or resets every time you power the unit on.
          </p>

          <p>
            A proper sleep mode does more than dim the display. On the Levoit Classic 300S and Dual 200S, sleep mode turns off the display and indicator lights and switches to a quiet automatic misting mode, so the machine ramps itself down once the room reaches a comfortable level. That combination of darkness, quiet and automatic control is what makes a humidifier pleasant to sleep next to rather than merely tolerable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Night lights are a matter of taste. For a child's room, a soft built-in light can double as a nightlight and save a separate plug-in. For an adult bedroom, most people prefer none. The Pure Enrichment MistAire's night light is optional, which is the right design. Look for amber or warm-colored lights if you want one at all, since blue and white light are the most disruptive at night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smart features are useful in a bedroom mainly for scheduling. Being able to start the humidifier an hour before bed so the room is comfortable when you get in, then have it switch to sleep mode automatically, is genuinely convenient. App control, voice assistants and remote monitoring are nice to have but not essential, and a simple machine with a timer and an automatic shut-off is perfectly adequate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cleaning: The Part That Decides Whether It Helps
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dirty humidifier is worse than no humidifier at all. Standing water in a warm room is an ideal place for bacteria and mold to grow, and an ultrasonic machine will happily spread whatever is growing in its tank into the air you breathe all night. That is not a reason to avoid humidifiers, but it is the reason cleaning ease is one of the four criteria behind every pick on this list.
          </p>

          <p>
            The routine is simple. Empty the tank every day rather than topping it up, rinse it, and refill with fresh water. Once a week, clean the tank and base thoroughly following the manufacturer's instructions, which usually means soaking with white vinegar to dissolve scale, then rinsing, and sometimes a diluted bleach or hydrogen peroxide rinse to disinfect. Always rinse well afterwards, and never run cleaning solution through the machine. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-a-humidifier/">how to clean a humidifier</a> guide covers each step for ultrasonic and evaporative units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Design makes a big difference to how likely you are to keep up with it. Top-fill tanks, like those on the Levoit and Dreo picks, let you reach inside with a brush and avoid the narrow neck of older bottle-style tanks. Wide openings dry out properly between uses. Dishwasher-safe parts, which the Canopy offers for its tank, cap and tray, make the weekly clean nearly effortless. Machines with hard-to-reach corners and small openings are the ones that end up growing film inside.
          </p>

          <p>
            Evaporative units add filter maintenance. The wick or filter traps minerals and needs replacing on the maker's schedule, typically every four to six weeks with daily use, sooner with hard water. A filter that is stiff, crusted or smells musty has stopped working well and should be replaced immediately. Factor that cost in before you buy: filters are the evaporative equivalent of an ultrasonic unit's distilled water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="match" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Match a Pick to Your Bedroom
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one answer, the Levoit Classic 300S is it. It has the capacity to run for days in a typical bedroom, a sleep mode that is genuinely dark and quiet, a top-fill tank that is easy to clean, and an automatic humidity mode that stops it over-humidifying the room. Use distilled water and it is close to ideal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For light sleepers who want the lowest published noise rating at a lower price, the Dreo HM311 is the better choice, and the Levoit Dual 200S is the right size for a small bedroom, a child's room or a guest room. If budget is the main constraint, the Pure Enrichment MistAire will do the job in a small room, provided you pair it with a hygrometer so you know when to turn it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have hard water and do not want to buy distilled water, go evaporative. The Honeywell HCM-350 is the long-running choice for a medium to large bedroom, and the Canopy Bedside is the compact nightstand option whose dishwasher-safe parts make it the easiest machine here to keep clean. Both make some fan noise, and both need filters, but neither leaves white dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the habits matter as much as the machine: keep humidity between 30 and 50 percent, empty and rinse the tank daily, clean it weekly, and keep the nozzle pointed away from the bed. For the full picture across every room in the house, see our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pointing the Mist at the Bed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mist that lands on bedding, a headboard or your pillow before it evaporates leaves things damp, and damp bedding is a mold risk. Keep the nozzle a few feet from the bed and point it into open air in the middle of the room, not towards you or the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Hard Tap Water in an Ultrasonic Unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ultrasonic humidifiers turn the minerals in tap water into a fine white dust that settles on furniture and electronics. Use distilled water, or choose an evaporative model whose filter traps the minerals instead of spreading them around the room.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping Up Instead of Emptying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adding fresh water on top of yesterday's leaves stagnant water in the tank to grow bacteria and film. Empty, rinse and refill the tank every day, and clean it properly once a week following the manual, before anything has a chance to grow.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It With No Hygrometer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a humidity reading, it is easy to push a closed bedroom past 50 percent, which feeds dust mites and mold and fogs the windows. A cheap hygrometer tells you when to turn it down, especially on models with no built-in sensor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Living-Room Machine for a Small Bedroom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oversized humidifier in a small closed room over-humidifies quickly and runs at settings that are louder than they need to be. Match the coverage rating to the room's floor area and treat the rating as a ceiling, not a target.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

