        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best humidifier for a bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most bedrooms, the Levoit Classic 300S is our pick. Levoit lists a 6-liter top-fill tank rated for up to 60 hours on low, a noise rating of 30 dB or less, and a sleep mode that turns off the display and night light. For small rooms, the Levoit Dual 200S is the better size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it good to sleep with a humidifier on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, yes, as long as humidity stays between about 30 and 50 percent and the machine is clean. It can ease dry mouth, scratchy throats and dry skin in winter. Keep it a few feet from the bed, empty it daily, and use a hygrometer so the room does not get too humid.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far should a humidifier be from your bed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for at least a few feet, with the nozzle pointing into the room rather than at the bed. Mist that lands on bedding before it evaporates leaves it damp. Place the unit on a raised, level surface such as a dresser and keep it a foot or more from walls and windows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is quieter, ultrasonic or evaporative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ultrasonic humidifiers are usually quieter because they do not need a large fan; the quietest picks here are rated at 28 dB. Evaporative units always make some fan noise, which some sleepers like as white noise. If you are a light sleeper, choose ultrasonic and use distilled water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a humidifier help with dry mouth at night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can help when the dryness comes from dry winter air, which is common in heated bedrooms. It will not fix dry mouth caused by medication, mouth breathing from a blocked nose, or sleep apnea. If dry mouth or loud snoring persists, talk to a doctor rather than relying on a humidifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size humidifier do I need for a bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the coverage rating to the room's floor area. A 1.5 to 3 liter unit suits rooms under 150 square feet, 3 to 4 liters suits 150 to 300 square feet, and 4 to 6 liters suits larger primary bedrooms. Size up for vaulted ceilings or drafty rooms and down for small closed ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use distilled water in a bedroom humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In an ultrasonic humidifier, yes. Tap water minerals become white dust on furniture and build up as scale inside the machine. Distilled water prevents both. Evaporative humidifiers can run on tap water because the filter traps the minerals, though the filter will need replacing more often with hard water.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

